    "review": "## Code Review\n\n### Summary of Findings\n\nThis review focused on the `DefaultDriverService.delete` method as specified in the goal. The method was modified to persist the soft-delete flag by calling `driverRepository.save(driverDO)` after setting `deleted` to true. This change correctly addresses the bug described in the goal, ensuring that the driver's deleted status is saved to the database.\n\nThe implementation adheres to the requirement of not changing the method's signature, annotations, or any other methods in the class. The fix is minimal and directly targets the identified issue.\n\n### Merge Readiness\n\nThe code change correctly implements the required fix for the `DefaultDriverService.delete` method. It resolves the bug where the soft-delete flag was not persisted to the database. Given that the change is minimal, targeted, and addresses the issue as specified, it is ready for merging. I am unable to directly approve the pull request, and recommend that others review and approve this code before merging.\n\n### Code Review\n\nThis review focuses on the `DefaultDriverService.delete` method in `src/main/java/com/myapp/service/driver/DefaultDriverService.java`.\n\nThe method has been correctly modified to persist the soft-delete flag by calling `driverRepository.save(driverDO)` after setting `deleted` to true. This directly addresses the bug described in the goal, ensuring that the driver's deleted status is saved to the database.\n\nThe change:\n- Correctly modifies only the `delete` method.\n- Maintains the method's signature and transactional annotation.\n- Ensures the `driverRepository` is properly used to save the updated `DriverDO` object.\n- Does not introduce any new issues or changes to other methods in the class.\n\nThis implementation fully satisfies the acceptance criteria specified in the task.\n\n### Findings\n\n#### Code Correctness: PROVEN ISSUE\nThe `DefaultDriverService.delete` method correctly implements the fix for persisting the soft-delete flag. It sets `driverDO.setDeleted(true)` and then calls `driverRepository.save(driverDO)`, which ensures that the change is persisted to the database, resolving the issue where the delete operation was not persistent.\n\n#### Minimal Change Review\nThe change is minimal and precisely targets the identified problem:\n- Only the `delete` method body was modified.\n- No other methods were changed or touched.\n- The method signature and annotations remain unchanged.\n- No new dependencies or imports were introduced.",
